---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TextToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/TextToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="text-to-pdf">
  <TextToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to turn text into a PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type or paste your text into the editor above, or drop a <code>.txt</code>, <code>.log</code>, <code>.csv</code> or <code>.md</code> file to load its contents.</li>
      <li>Choose a font, size, line height, page size and margin. The counters show the words, lines and characters in your text.</li>
      <li>Turn on page numbers for a long document.</li>
      <li>Set the file name, press <strong>Create PDF</strong>, and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Choosing a font</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Font</th>
            <th>Character</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>Words per A4 page at 11 pt</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Helvetica</td>
            <td>Neutral sans serif, even colour on the page</td>
            <td>Notes, letters, handouts, anything read on screen</td>
            <td>≈ 500</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Times</td>
            <td>Serif, narrower letterforms</td>
            <td>Long prose, essays, printed documents</td>
            <td>≈ 600</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Courier</td>
            <td>Monospaced: every character has the same width</td>
            <td>Logs, code, fixed-width tables, ASCII art</td>
            <td>≈ 350</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      All three are among the PDF standard's base 14 fonts, so they are not embedded in the file. A fifty-page
      document is usually under 100 KB, and it looks the same in every reader on every operating system.
    </p>

    <h2>How the text is laid out</h2>
    <p>
      Word wrapping uses the real metrics of the chosen font, not a character count, so lines fill the column
      without breaking early or overflowing:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Words break at spaces, and the line is filled greedily up to the column width.</li>
      <li>A single token wider than the column, such as a long URL, a base64 blob or a checksum, is split by character so it cannot run off the page.</li>
      <li>Hard line breaks in your input stay exactly where they are, which keeps logs, poetry and code readable.</li>
      <li>Blank lines become vertical space, so paragraphs stay visually separated.</li>
      <li>Tabs are expanded to four spaces, because PDF has no tab stops of its own.</li>
      <li>At the bottom margin a new page starts. Page numbers, if on, are added at the end, when the total is known.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Margins and readability</h2>
    <p>
      A comfortable line length is about 60 to 80 characters. On A4 at 11 pt Helvetica, a 20 mm margin gives
      about 85 characters per line: fine for reference material, slightly long for continuous reading. For a
      document read from start to finish, raise the margin to 30 mm, or keep 20 mm and use a 12 pt font. Line
      height matters as much: 1.4 to 1.5 suits body text, and 1.15 to 1.25 suits dense logs where you want
      more on each page.
    </p>

    <h2>Which characters can be used</h2>
    <p>
      The standard PDF fonts use WinAnsi encoding, which covers the Latin alphabet, accented Western European
      letters, curly quotes, en and em dashes, the euro sign and common symbols. Characters outside that set
      (Cyrillic, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, CJK, emoji) need an embedded font file. The text to PDF converter
      transliterates what it safely can, by removing diacritics and mapping arrows and mathematical operators
      to ASCII, and reports how many characters it replaced, so you can decide whether the output is usable.
    </p>

    <h2>Reasons to make a PDF from text</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sending a text that must not be edited or reflowed, such as a quote, a statement or a set of instructions.</li>
      <li>Printing plain text with real margins and page breaks, in place of a browser's print stylesheet.</li>
      <li>Attaching logs to a ticket in a format that opens the same way for everyone.</li>
      <li>Archiving notes in a self-contained format that will still open in twenty years.</li>
      <li>Making a clean handout from notes without a word processor.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For Markdown, <a href="/markdown-to-pdf">Markdown to PDF</a> keeps your headings, lists and code blocks.
      For the reverse direction, use <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to Text</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
